From ec78f60c9823601ffa3b3ae64fcda0e2e3a434c9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: leo <leoschlosser@tutamail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 22:13:42 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 91/92] power: supply: max77693: use 460 mA input limit on USB
 ports

On the Galaxy Note II (GT-N7100) connected to a PC, the 500 mA input limit
used for SDP/CDP/slow chargers puts CHGIN into UVLO immediately
(CHG_DTLS_00.CHGIN_DTLS = 0, CHG_INT_OK.CHGIN_OK = 0) and it stays there,
so the battery never charges and the power supply reports offline.
Stepping CHG_CNFG_09 shows 460 mA is the highest limit that keeps CHGIN
valid; Samsung's vendor kernel uses 360 mA.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/power/supply/max77693_charger.c | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/power/supply/max77693_charger.c b/drivers/power/supply/max77693_charger.c
index 6049f3f0c..cef23f615 100644
--- a/drivers/power/supply/max77693_charger.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/max77693_charger.c
@@ -731,7 +731,13 @@ static void max77693_charger_extcon_work(struct work_struct *work)
 	case EXTCON_CHG_USB_SDP:
 	case EXTCON_CHG_USB_CDP:
 	case EXTCON_CHG_USB_SLOW:
-		input_current = 500000; /* 500 mA */
+		/*
+		 * Not quite the 500 mA USB allows: on the Galaxy Note II (with a
+		 * PC port) an input limit of 480 mA or more makes CHGIN drop into
+		 * UVLO right away and stay there, so nothing charges. Samsung's
+		 * kernel uses 360 mA; 460 mA is the highest that keeps CHGIN valid.
+		 */
+		input_current = 460000;
 		set_charging = true;
 		set_otg = false;
 
